image,name,country,years,house/party,role,excerpt
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/Augustus_of_Prima_Porta.jpg,Augustus,rome,27 BC - 14 AD,julio-claudian,emperor,First Roman Emperor. Established Pax Romana and transformed Republic into Empire.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/8095_-_Roma_-_Ara_Pacis_-_Tiberio_-_Foto_Giovanni_Dall%27Orto_-_28-Mar-2008.jpg,Tiberius,rome,14-37 AD,julio-claudian,emperor,Second emperor. Initially capable ruler who withdrew to Capri in paranoia.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/Caligula.Carlsberg_Glyptotek.%28cropped%29.jpg,Caligula,rome,37-41 AD,julio-claudian,emperor,Third emperor. Infamous for cruelty and erratic behavior. Murdered after 4 years.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/Claudius_crop.jpg,Claudius,rome,41-54 AD,julio-claudian,emperor,Fourth emperor. Despite disabilities proved capable administrator. Conquered Britain.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Nero_Glyptothek_Munich_321.jpg,Nero,rome,54-68 AD,julio-claudian,emperor,Fifth emperor. Infamous for tyranny. Persecuted Christians. Committed suicide.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e6/Gold_Aureus_of_Galba_%28MANTIS%29.jpg,Galba,rome,68-69 AD,galba,emperor,Sixth emperor. Military coup. Ruled only 7 months before assassination.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5e/Statue_of_the_emperor_Otho%2C_Ma_1215_%28cropped1%29.jpg,Otho,rome,69 AD,vitellius,emperor,Seventh emperor. Ruled 95 days during Year of Four Emperors. Defeated by Vitellius.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Gold_Aureus_of_Vitellius_%28MANTIS%29.jpg,Vitellius,rome,69 AD,vitellius,emperor,Eighth emperor. Lasted only 69 days. Murdered by Vespasian's troops.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Vespasian%2C_from_Naples%2C_c._AD_70%2C_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek%2C_Copenhagen_%2813646730625%29.jpg,Vespasian,rome,69-79 AD,flavian,emperor,Ninth emperor. Restored stability after civil war. Began Colosseum construction.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/Titus_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek_IN3159.jpg,Titus,rome,79-81 AD,flavian,emperor,Tenth emperor. Brief reign. Completed Colosseum. Besieged Jerusalem.
,Domitian,rome,81-96 AD,flavian,emperor,Eleventh emperor. Authoritarian rule. Demanded to be called master and god. Assassinated.
,Nerva,rome,96-98 AD,nerva,emperor,Twelfth emperor. Started practice of adopting capable successor instead of heredity.
,Trajan,rome,98-117 AD,antonine,emperor,Thirteenth emperor. Expanded empire to greatest extent. Known as optimus princeps (best ruler).
,Hadrian,rome,117-138 AD,antonine,emperor,Fourteenth emperor. Consolidated rather than expanded. Built Hadrian's Wall and Pantheon.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ca/Antoninus_Pius_bust_S2436_ancient_agora_museum_Athens.jpg,Antoninus Pius,rome,138-161 AD,antonine,emperor,Fifteenth emperor. Most peaceful period of empire. Known for justice and piety.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/MSR-ra-61-b-1-DM.jpg,Marcus Aurelius,rome,161-180 AD,antonine,emperor,Sixteenth emperor. Stoic philosopher-emperor. Authored Meditations. Faced plague and war.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Commodo_vestito_da_Ercole_ai_Musei_Capitolini.jpg,Commodus,rome,180-192 AD,antonine,emperor,Seventeenth emperor. Erratic tyrant. Obsessed with gladiatorial combat. Assassinated by courtiers.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Galleria_degli_Uffizi%2C_Florence_%2819676748662%29.jpg,Pertinax,rome,193 AD,severan,emperor,Eighteenth emperor. Attempted reforms. Assassinated after 87 days.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/Buste_de_Didius_Iulianus5.jpg,Didius Julianus,rome,193 AD,severan,emperor,Nineteenth emperor. Purchased throne from Praetorian Guard. Lasted 66 days before execution.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/Septimius_Severus_busto-Musei_Capitolini.jpg,Septimius Severus,rome,193-211 AD,severan,emperor,Twentieth emperor. Military strongman who centralized power. Founded Severan dynasty.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/0_Caracalla_-_Museo_Massimo_alle_Terme.JPG,Caracalla,rome,211-217 AD,severan,emperor,Twenty-first emperor. Granted citizenship to all free inhabitants. Murdered by his praetorian prefect.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Bust_of_Macrinus_-_Palazzo_Nuovo_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg,Macrinus,rome,217-218 AD,severan,emperor,Twenty-second emperor. First of non-senatorial origin. Brief unstable reign.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/Bronze-Uranius_Antoninus-Elagabal_stone-SGI_4414.jpg,Elagabal,rome,218-222 AD,severan,emperor,Twenty-third emperor. Youngest ever at 14. Reign marked by scandal. Assassinated.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1d/Alexander_Severus_Musei_Capitolini_MC471.jpg,Severus Alexander,rome,222-235 AD,severan,emperor,Twenty-fourth emperor. Dominated by his mother Julia Maesa. Assassination marked start of Third Century Crisis.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Maximinus_Thrax_Musei_Capitolini_MC473.jpg,Maximinus Thrax,rome,235-238 AD,severan,emperor,Twenty-fifth emperor. First barbarian emperor. Capable military commander. Killed during siege.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Gordian1cng80000719_%28obverse%29.jpg,Gordian I,rome,238 AD,gordian,emperor,Twenty-sixth emperor. Proclaimed at age 79. Died within days.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4e/Gordiano_II_-_ritratto_da_sesterzio.jpg,Gordian II,rome,238 AD,gordian,emperor,Twenty-seventh emperor. Son of Gordian I. Died in battle with father.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/79/Pupienus_Musei_Capitolini_MC477.jpg,Pupienus,rome,238 AD,gordian,emperor,Twenty-eighth emperor. Proclaimed by Senate. Murdered after 79 days.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/Balbinus_Hermitage.jpg,Balbinus,rome,238 AD,gordian,emperor,Twenty-ninth emperor. Co-ruled with Pupienus. Both murdered by Praetorian Guard.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Bust_Gordianus_III_Louvre_Ma1063.jpg,Gordian III,rome,238-244 AD,gordian,emperor,Thirtieth emperor. Became ruler at age 13. Died during Persian campaign.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Portrait_of_Philip_the_Arab._Saint-Petersburg%2C_The_State_Hermitage_Museum_%28cropped%29.jpg,Philip the Arab,rome,244-249 AD,philip,emperor,Thirty-first emperor. First Arab emperor. Pursued peace with Persia. Killed in civil war.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/79/Bust_of_Decius_%28loan_from_Capitoline_Museums%29_-_Glyptothek_-_Munich_-_Germany_2017.jpg,Decius,rome,249-251 AD,decius,emperor,Thirty-second emperor. Persecuted Christians. Died in battle against Goths.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Bronze_statue_of_the_emperor_Trebonianus_Gallus_%28detail%29.jpg,Trebonianus Gallus,rome,251-253 AD,decius,emperor,Thirty-third emperor. Paid tribute to Gothic invaders. Deposed.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9f/Aemilianus_RIC_IV_20_%28obverse%29.jpg,Aemilianus,rome,253 AD,decius,emperor,Thirty-fourth emperor. Ruled only months. Defeated by Valerian.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/Valerianus_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek_IN3387.jpg,Valerian,rome,253-260 AD,valerian,emperor,Thirty-fifth emperor. Defeated at Battle of Edessa. Captured by Persians.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/Ritratto_di_gallieno_dalla_casa_delle_vestali.jpg,Gallienus,rome,260-268 AD,valerian,emperor,Thirty-sixth emperor. Ruled while father was captive. Empire fragmented. Assassinated.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/ClaudiusGothicusSC265569.jpg,Claudius Gothicus,rome,268-270 AD,valerian,emperor,Thirty-seventh emperor. Defeated Germanic invaders. Gained fame as Gothicus.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Aureus_Quintillus_%28obverse%29.jpg,Quintillus,rome,270 AD,valerian,emperor,Thirty-eighth emperor. Proclaimed by Senate. Murdered by Aurelian's troops.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8a/Aureus_of_Aurelian_%28Milan%29_%28obverse%29.jpg,Aurelian,rome,270-275 AD,illyrian,emperor,Thirty-ninth emperor. Restored unity of Roman Empire through military campaigns.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/Wien-_Parlament-Tacitus.jpg,Tacitus,rome,275-276 AD,illyrian,emperor,Fortieth emperor. Proclaimed by Senate. Lasted only 6 months.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/Fl%C3%B3ri%C3%A1n_Albert_cropped.jpg,Florian,rome,276 AD,illyrian,,Forty-first emperor. Deposed by Probus after only months.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Probus_Musei_Capitolini_MC493.jpg,Probus,rome,276-282 AD,illyrian,emperor,Forty-second emperor. Defeated barbarian invasions. Restored Roman control. Murdered by soldiers.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/Monnaie_-_Aureus%2C_Carus%2C_Siscia_-_btv1b11344796d_%281_of_2%29.jpg,Carus,rome,282-283 AD,carus,emperor,Forty-third emperor. Capable military commander. Died in battle against Persians.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f5/NumerianusAntoninianus.jpg,Numerian,rome,283-284 AD,carus,emperor,Forty-fourth emperor. Co-ruled with brother. Murdered during Persian retreat.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Montemartini_-_Carino_1030439.JPG,Carinus,rome,283-285 AD,carus,emperor,Forty-fifth emperor. Co-ruled in west. Defeated by Diocletian in civil war.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Istanbul_Archaeology_Museum_Bust_of_Diocletian%2C_from_Nikomedia%2C_Izmit%2C_end_3rd_century_AD_1536.jpg,Diocletian,rome,284-305 AD,tetrarchy,emperor,Forty-sixth emperor. Established Tetrarchy system dividing empire into four parts. Voluntarily retired.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Mus%C3%A9e_Saint-Raymond_-_2017-09-02_-_Inv._Ra_34b_-_4654.jpg,Maximian,rome,286-305 AD,tetrarchy,emperor,Co-emperor with Diocletian ruling western empire. Defeated Germanic invaders. Retired with Diocletian.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d6/Constantius_Chlorus_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek_IN836.jpg,Constantius Chlorus,rome,293-306 AD,tetrarchy,emperor,Caesar under Diocletian. Recovered Britain. Died as Augustus of west.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/99/Romuliana_Galerius_head.jpg,Galerius,rome,293-311 AD,tetrarchy,emperor,Caesar under Diocletian. Persecuted Christians. Issued toleration edict before death.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c8/Statua_di_Costantino_ai_musei_capitolini.jpg,Constantine I,rome,306-337 AD,constantine,emperor,Forty-seventh emperor. Won Battle of Milvian Bridge. Established Christianity as preferred religion. Founded Constantinople.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/Bust_of_Licinius%2C_Kunsthistorisches_Museum.jpg,Licinius,rome,308-324 AD,constantine,emperor,Constantine's rival in east. Defeated in civil wars. Executed.
,Constantine II,rome,337-340 AD,constantine,consul,Constantine's son. Co-ruled with brothers. Killed in civil war after 3 years.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_-_L%27empereur_Constant_%28Ma_1021%29.jpg,Constans,rome,340-350 AD,constantine,emperor,Constantine's son. Inherited west and center. Overthrown and murdered by Magnentius.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/30/Bust_of_Constantius_II_%28Mary_Harrsch%29.jpg,Constantius II,rome,337-361 AD,constantine,emperor,Constantine's son. Inherited east then entire empire. Faced religious conflict and invasions.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Medallion_of_Magnentius.png,Magnentius,rome,350-353 AD,constantine,roman usurper,Usurper in west. Murdered Constans. Defeated by Constantius II. Committed suicide.
,Julian,rome,361-363 AD,julian,,Last pagan emperor. Attempted to restore classical religion. Died in Persian campaign.
,Jovian,rome,363-364 AD,julian,emperor,Restored Christianity. Died under mysterious circumstances after months.
,Valentinian I,rome,364-375 AD,valentinian,emperor,Strong military commander. Defended frontiers. Split empire with brother Valens.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fc/INC-3015-a_%D0%A1%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%B4._%D0%92%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%82_II._%D0%9E%D0%BA._364_%D0%B3._%28%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%81%29.png,Valens,rome,364-378 AD,valentinian,emperor,Ruled eastern empire. Made alliance with Goths. Died at Battle of Adrianople.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Gratian_Trier_enhanced.jpg,Gratian,rome,375-383 AD,valentinian,emperor,Valentinian I's son. Lost support to Magnus Maximus. Murdered.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Bust_of_Theodosius_I.jpg,Theodosius I,rome,379-395 AD,theodosius,emperor,Last emperor to rule entire empire. Made Christianity official state religion. Defeated Goths.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/70/Rare_aureus_of_Magnus_Maximus_%28obverse_transparent%29.png,Magnus Maximus,rome,383-388 AD,theodosian,emperor,Usurper in west. Defeated Gratian. Defeated by Theodosius in civil war.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/Eugenius_coin_%28transparent%29.png,Eugenius,rome,392-394 AD,theodosian,consul,Usurper in west. Defeated and killed by Theodosius at Battle of Frigidus River.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/Diptych_of_Honorius_%28head%29_%282%29.jpg,Honorius,western rome,395-423 AD,theodosian,consul,Last reigning western Roman emperor. Saw barbarian invasions including Visigoths sacking Rome.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Arcadius_Istanbul_Museum.JPG,Arcadius,eastern rome,395-408 AD,theodosian,emperor,Eastern Roman emperor. Dominated by advisors. Faced civil strife and military threats.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bb/Mapa_Imperio_Occidental_a%C3%B1o_418.svg,John (Usurper),western rome,423-425 AD,theodosian,emperor,Military usurper. Ruled only 2 years before defeat by Valentinian III's forces.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Bust_of_Valentinian_III%2C_Louvre_%28cropped%29.jpg,Valentinian III,western rome,425-455 AD,theodosian,ancient roman senator,Final reigning western Roman emperor. Reign saw continued barbarian invasions. Death marked effective empire's end.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/Solidus_of_Petronius_Maximus.png,Petronius Maximus,western rome,455 AD,theodosian,consul,Ruled only 75 days before being murdered during Vandal sack of Rome. Exemplifies late western chaos.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Solidus_Avitus_Arles_%28obverse%29.jpg,Avitus,western rome,455-456 AD,theodosian,consul,Proclaimed by Visigothic king. Deposed after one year. Reign contested and unstable.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Solidus_Majorian_Arles_%28obverse%29.jpg,Majorian,western rome,457-461 AD,theodosian,ancient roman senator,Capable military commander who attempted to restore authority. Faced increasing barbarian pressure.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Libiusseverus01854obverse.jpg,Libius Severus,western rome,461-465 AD,theodosian,ancient roman senator,Weak emperor dominated by magister militum Ricimer. Further decline of imperial authority.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/62/Solidus_of_Anthemius.png,Anthemius,western rome,467-472 AD,theodosian,consul,Appointed by eastern empire. Conflict with Ricimer. Murdered by Ricimer's forces.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/Tremissis_Olybrius_%28obverse%29.jpg,Olybrius,western rome,472 AD,theodosian,consul,Elevated by Ricimer. Ruled only months. One of shortest reigns.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9b/Gold_Solidus_of_Glycerius.jpg,Glycerius,western rome,473-474 AD,theodosian,emperor,Proclaimed emperor. Quickly deposed by Odoacer. Symbols ending of legitimate succession.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Solidus_of_Julius_Nepos.png,Julius Nepos,western rome,474-475 AD,theodosian,emperor,Appointed by eastern empire. Deposed by Orestes. Claimed title in exile.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Gold_Solidus_of_Romulus_Augustulus.jpg,Romulus Augustulus,western rome,475-476 AD,theodosian,emperor,Last western Roman emperor. Deposed by Odoacer in 476 marking traditional end of Western Roman Empire.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/Konstantinos_XI_Palaiologos_fresco_%28less_edited%29.jpg,Leo I,eastern rome/byzantine,457-474 AD,theodosian,pope,Eastern emperor. Defended Constantinople against Arab siege. Reinforced eastern imperial authority.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8b/Solidus_of_Leo_II.png,Leo II,eastern rome/byzantine,474-475 AD,theodosian,emperor,Child emperor. Ruled with grandfather. Died at age 7.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg,Zeno,eastern rome/byzantine,475-491 AD,theodosian,emperor,Reclaimed throne after Leo II. Faced Ostrogothic invasion of Italy. Worked to restore prestige.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Solidus_of_emperor_Basiliscus.jpg,Basiliscus,eastern rome/byzantine,475-476 AD,theodosian,emperor,Usurper. Briefly deposed Zeno. Lasted less than year before Zeno's restoration.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Flavius_Anastasius_Probus_01c_%28Anastasius_I%29_%28cropped%29.JPG,Anastasius I,eastern rome/byzantine,491-518 AD,theodosian,emperor,Elected by aristocracy after Zeno. Brought prosperity. Reformed currency and taxation.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Solidus_of_Justin_I_%28obverse%29.jpg,Justin I,eastern rome/byzantine,518-527 AD,theodosian,emperor,Military officer who became emperor. Appointed nephew Justinian as co-emperor.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3d/Mosaic_of_Justinianus_I_-_Basilica_San_Vitale_%28Ravenna%29.jpg,Justinian I,eastern rome/byzantine,527-565 AD,theodosian,emperor,One of greatest Byzantine emperors. Conquered North Africa and southern Europe. Created Justinian Code. Faced Nika Riots.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Solidus_of_Justin_II_%28obverse%29.jpg,Justin II,eastern rome/byzantine,565-578 AD,theodosian,emperor,Inherited empire at height. Faced military pressures. Mental illness affected later governance.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Tiberios_II_%28obverse%29.jpg,Tiberius II,eastern rome/byzantine,578-582 AD,theodosian,emperor,Became co-emperor. Military reforms against Avars and Slavs on Balkans.
,Maurice,eastern rome/byzantine,582-602 AD,theodosian,,Capable military leader. Renewed strength against Persia. Murdered in coup.
,Phocas,eastern rome/byzantine,602-610 AD,theodosian,emperor,Seized power in coup. Reign marked by religious conflict and military defeats.
,Heraclius,eastern rome/byzantine,610-641 AD,theodosian,emperor,Great military reformer. Defeated Persians. Recovered True Cross. Faced rise of Islam.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg,Constantine III,eastern rome/byzantine,641 AD,theodosian,emperor,Co-emperor with Heraclius II. Reign lasted only months before death from plague.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/Heraclius_solidus.jpg,Heraclius II,eastern rome/byzantine,641 AD,theodosian,emperor,Son of Heraclius. Reign months before deposition by Constans II.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Solidus_Constans_II_%28obverse%29.jpg,Constans II,eastern rome/byzantine,641-668 AD,theodosian,emperor,Lost Egypt and Syria to Arab conquests. Murdered in Sicily. Reign saw eastern losses.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fb/Constantine_IV_mosaic.png,Constantine IV,eastern rome/byzantine,668-685 AD,theodosian,emperor,Successfully defended Constantinople against Arab siege. Stabilized eastern frontier.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d1/Justinian_ii_%282%29.png,Justinian II,eastern rome/byzantine,685-695 & 705-711 AD,theodosian,emperor,"Known as Rhinotmetus (slit-nosed) for mutilation by usurpers. First reign victories, second restored authority."
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Solidus_of_Leontius.png,Leontius,eastern rome/byzantine,695-698 AD,theodosian,emperor,Usurper. Mutilated Justinian II. Deposed by Tiberius III.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Tiberius_III_solidus-2_%28cropped%29.png,Tiberius III,eastern rome/byzantine,698-705 AD,theodosian,emperor,Deposed Leontius. Overcome by Justinian II's restoration.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Solidus_of_Philippicus.png,Philippicus Bardanes,eastern rome/byzantine,711-713 AD,theodosian,emperor,Usurper. Brief reign. Religious controversy over Monothelitism.
,Anastasius II,eastern rome/byzantine,713-715 AD,theodosian,emperor,Attempted military reforms. Deposed by Theodosius III.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/Coin_of_Theodosius_III.png,Theodosius III,eastern rome/byzantine,715-717 AD,theodosian,emperor,Usurper. Deposed by Leo III in less than two years.
,Leo III,eastern rome/byzantine,717-741 AD,theodosian,emperor,Defeated Arab siege of Constantinople. Military recovery. Beginning of Iconoclasm.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/Solidus_of_Constantine_V_%28transparent_background%29.png,Constantine V,eastern rome/byzantine,741-775 AD,theodosian,emperor,Capable military commander. Victories against Arabs and Bulgars. Ardent Iconoclast.
,Leo IV,eastern rome/byzantine,775-780 AD,theodosian,emperor,Constantine V's son. Continued military campaigns. Died at age 24.
,Constantine VI,eastern rome/byzantine,780-797 AD,theodosian,emperor,Child emperor ruled by mother Irene. Restoration of icon worship. Blinded by his mother.
,Irene,eastern rome/byzantine,797-802 AD,theodosian,emperor,First woman to rule Byzantine Empire as sole emperor. Restored icon worship.
,Nicephorus I,eastern rome/byzantine,802-811 AD,theodosian,emperor,Capable administrator and military leader. Died in battle against Bulgars.
,Staurakios,eastern rome/byzantine,811 AD,theodosian,empress,Ruled only months. Illness and disability led to abdication.
,Michael I,eastern rome/byzantine,811-813 AD,theodosian,,Chosen after Staurakios. Defeated by Bulgars. Abdicated to become monk.
,Leo V,eastern rome/byzantine,813-820 AD,theodosian,,Anatolian general. Restored Iconoclasm. Murdered by co-conspirators.
,Michael II,eastern rome/byzantine,820-829 AD,theodosian,emperor,Seized power after Leo V's death. Rise of Amorian dynasty.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/Patriarch_Theophilos_III_of_Jerusalem_Senate_of_Poland_01.JPG,Theophilus,eastern rome/byzantine,829-842 AD,theodosian,,Capable military commander. Amorian war against Abbasids. Maintained Iconoclasm.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg,Theodora,eastern rome/byzantine,842-856 AD,theodosian,emperor,Regent for young son Michael III. Restoration of icon worship. Deposed when son came of age.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg,Michael III,eastern rome/byzantine,842-867 AD,theodosian,emperor,Known as the Drunkard. Reconquered Crete. Converted Bulgaria. Murdered by Basil I.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9f/Roman_Emperor_Basil_I.png,Basil I,eastern rome/byzantine,867-886 AD,theodosian,emperor,Capable administrator. Founded Macedonian Renaissance. Reformed administrative and legal systems.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg,Leo VI,eastern rome/byzantine,886-912 AD,theodosian,emperor,Highly educated. Military struggles with Arabs and Bulgars. Legal compilations became important texts.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Istanbul_-_Museo_archeol._-_Alessandro_Magno_%28firmata_Menas%29_-_sec._III_a.C._-_da_Magnesia_-_Foto_G._Dall%27Orto_28-5-2006_b-n.jpg,Alexander,eastern rome/byzantine,912-913 AD,theodosian,pharaoh,Leo VI's younger brother. Ruled only year. Conflict with patriarch.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/Constantine_VII_Porphyrogenitus_%282%29.jpeg,Constantine VII,eastern rome/byzantine,913-959 AD,theodosian,emperor,Child emperor regained after struggle. Stabilized frontier. Patron of literature and arts.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/Seal_of_Romanos_I_Lekapenos.png,Romanus I,eastern rome/byzantine,919-944 AD,theodosian,emperor,Co-emperor with Constantine VII. Military victories against Arabs. Deposed.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/Romanos_et_Eudoxie_%28cropped2%29.JPG,Romanus II,eastern rome/byzantine,959-963 AD,theodosian,emperor,Constantine VII's son. Brief reign with military successes. Died young.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/Nikephoros_Phokas.jpg,Nicephorus II,eastern rome/byzantine,963-969 AD,theodosian,emperor,Great military commander. Reconquests of Crete and Cyprus. Military prowess defined reign.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg,John I,eastern rome/byzantine,969-976 AD,theodosian,emperor,Outstanding military commander and administrator. Continued expansion. Death marked end of great period.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dc/Basilios_II.jpg,Basil II,eastern rome/byzantine,976-1025 AD,theodosian,emperor,Bulgar-Slayer. One of greatest emperors. Military campaigns expanded empire to greatest extent in centuries.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/88/Constantine_VIII_in_the_Exultet_roll_%282%29.jpg,Constantine VIII,eastern rome/byzantine,1025-1028 AD,theodosian,emperor,Co-ruled with Basil II. Politically inactive during brother's reign. Brief sole rule.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg,Zoe,eastern rome/byzantine,1028-1050 AD,theodosian,,Co-empress after Constantine VIII. Married multiple emperors. Reign saw stability.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5d/Romanos_III_in_Madrid_Skylitzes.png,Romanus III,eastern rome/byzantine,1028-1034 AD,theodosian,emperor,Married Zoe. Military setbacks. Possibly murdered by Zoe.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg,Michael IV,eastern rome/byzantine,1034-1041 AD,theodosian,emperor,Chosen by Zoe. Military campaigns in Sicily. Suffered from epilepsy. Abdicated.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg,Michael V,eastern rome/byzantine,1041-1042 AD,theodosian,emperor,Michael IV's adopted son. Attempted removal of Zoe. Deposed and blinded in popular uprising.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/94/Emperor_Constantine_IX.jpg,Constantine IX,eastern rome/byzantine,1042-1054 AD,theodosian,emperor,Married Zoe. Reign saw beginning of schism between eastern and western Christianity.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/78/Theodora_in_the_Madrid_Skylitzes2.png,Theodora II,eastern rome/byzantine,1054-1056 AD,theodosian,empress,Zoe's sister. Ruled after Constantine IX. Forced to abdicate.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Tetarteron_of_Michael_VI_%28reverse%29.png,Michael VI,eastern rome/byzantine,1056-1057 AD,theodosian,emperor,Chosen after Theodora. Marked decline. Abdicated due to military pressure.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d6/Constantine_X_full_portrait.jpg,Constantine X,eastern rome/byzantine,1057-1067 AD,theodosian,emperor,Chosen to restore military strength. Reign saw losses to Seljuks. Widow ruled as regent.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg,Eudokia,eastern rome/byzantine,1067-1071 AD,theodosian,empress,Regent for young Michael VII. Diplomatic efforts and military reforms. Abdicated.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Romanus_IV_coin_crop.png,Romanus IV,eastern rome/byzantine,1068-1071 AD,theodosian,emperor,Married Eudokia. Defeated at Manzikert by Seljuks. Deposed and imprisoned.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Michael_VII_Doukas_from_the_Khakhuli_Triptych.png,Michael VII,eastern rome/byzantine,1071-1078 AD,theodosian,emperor,Known as the Drunkard. Continued military losses. Ineffectiveness led to Comneni rise.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Nikephoros_III.jpg,Nicephorus III,eastern rome/byzantine,1078-1081 AD,theodosian,emperor,Chosen to restore military strength. Brief rule. Abdicated for Alexios I Komnenos.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Alexios_I_Komnenos.jpg,Alexios I,eastern rome/byzantine,1081-1118 AD,komnenus,emperor,Founded Komnenus dynasty. Recovery of Anatolia. Called for First Crusade. Stabilized empire.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg,John II,eastern rome/byzantine,1118-1143 AD,komnenus,king,Outstanding military commander. Recovered lost territories. Called John the Beautiful.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg,Manuel I,eastern rome/byzantine,1143-1180 AD,komnenus,emperor,Saw height of Komnene power. Military campaigns in Balkans and Mediterranean. Major power.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg,Alexios II,eastern rome/byzantine,1180-1183 AD,komnenus,,Child emperor. Regency crises and massacre of Latin merchants. Murdered by Andronikos I.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/Konstantinos_XI_Palaiologos_fresco_%28less_edited%29.jpg,Andronikos I,eastern rome/byzantine,1183-1185 AD,komnenus,emperor,Tyrannical ruler. Persecuted Latin community. Death in riot ended Komnene dynasty.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/Konstantinos_XI_Palaiologos_fresco_%28less_edited%29.jpg,Constantine XI,eastern rome/byzantine,1449-1453 AD,palaiologos,emperor,Last Byzantine emperor. Final Ottoman siege of Constantinople. Died defending the city.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/QinShiHuang19century.jpg,Qin Shi Huang,china,-221 to -210,qin,emperor,First emperor to unify China. Standardized weights and writing. Built Great Wall and Terracotta Army.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Qin_Er_Shi.png,Qin Er Shi,china,-210 to -207,qin,emperor,Second son of Qin Shi Huang. Reign saw decline of Qin authority and empire collapse. Murdered.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/Liu_Bang.jpg,Liu Bang (Emperor Gaozu),china,-202 to -195,han,emperor,Founded Han Dynasty. Established many institutions. Granted noble titles. Began reunification.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/Hui_man.jpg,Han Hui,china,-195 to -188,han,emperor,Dominated by mother Empress Lü. Her regency after his reign destroyed rival families.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/%E6%BC%A2%E6%AD%A6%E5%B8%9D.jpg,Han Wudi,china,-156 to -87,han,emperor,One of China's greatest emperors. Expanded empire. Established Silk Road trade routes. Secured borders.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/25/%E5%94%90%E5%90%8E%E8%A1%8C%E5%BE%9E%E5%9C%96_%28%E5%B1%80%E9%83%A8%29_-_%E6%AD%A6%E5%89%87%E5%A4%A9.png,Wu Zetian,china,690-705,zhou,emperor,Only woman to rule China as emperor. Rose through political acumen and ruthlessness. Territorial expansion.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/TangTaizong.jpg,Tang Taizong,china,626-649,tang,emperor,Brilliant military strategist. Expanded Tang Dynasty to greatest extent. Military victories. Reforms.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/%E6%B8%85%E5%9C%A3%E7%A5%96%E5%BA%B7%E7%86%99%E7%9A%87%E5%B8%9D%E6%9C%9D%E6%9C%8D%E5%83%8F.jpg,Kangxi Emperor,china,1661-1722,qing,emperor,One of China's greatest emperors. Conquered Taiwan. Extended control into Mongolia and Tibet. Patron of arts.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/80/%E9%9B%8D%E6%AD%A3%E6%9C%9D%E6%9C%8D%E5%83%8F%E8%BD%B4.jpg,Yongzheng Emperor,china,1722-1735,qing,emperor,Efficient administrator. Consolidated power. Suppressed corruption. Prepared for Qianlong's reign.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cd/%E4%B9%BE%E9%9A%86%E7%9A%87%E5%B8%9D%E6%9C%9D%E6%9C%8D%E5%83%8F%E8%BD%B4.png,Qianlong Emperor,china,1735-1796,qing,emperor,One of history's longest-reigning emperors. Extended dominion to greatest extent. Patron of arts and literature.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bb/%E6%B8%85_%E4%BD%9A%E5%90%8D_%E3%80%8A%E6%B8%85%E4%BB%81%E5%AE%97%E5%98%89%E5%BA%86%E7%9A%87%E5%B8%9D%E6%9C%9D%E6%9C%8D%E5%83%8F%E3%80%8B.jpg,Jiaqing Emperor,china,1796-1820,qing,emperor,Suppressed corruption. Attempted to reverse economic decline. Faced rebellions and territorial losses.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Emperor_Daoguang.jpg,Daoguang Emperor,china,1820-1850,qing,emperor,Oversaw China during Opium Wars. Attempted to stop opium trafficking. Military defeat by Britain marked decline.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Xianfeng.jpg,Xianfeng Emperor,china,1850-1861,qing,emperor,Presided during Second Opium War and Taiping Rebellion. Military defeats and territorial losses. Died during foreign occupation.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/%E6%B8%85_%E4%BD%9A%E5%90%8D_%E3%80%8A%E6%B8%85%E7%A9%86%E5%AE%97%E5%90%8C%E6%B2%BB%E7%9A%87%E5%B8%9D%E6%9C%9D%E6%9C%8D%E5%83%8F%E3%80%8B.jpg,Tongzhi Emperor,china,1861-1875,qing,emperor,Child emperor. Saw recentralization of Qing power under Empress Dowager. Died young.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Guangxu.jpg,Guangxu Emperor,china,1875-1908,qing,emperor,Attempted modernization reforms. Empress Dowager Cixi suppressed reforms. Died under suspicious circumstances.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/%E5%AD%99%E4%B8%AD%E5%B1%B1%E8%82%96%E5%83%8F.jpg,Sun Yat-sen,china,1912-1925,kuomintang,,Led Chinese Revolution. Overthrew Qing Dynasty. Father of Chinese Republic.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/%E8%94%A3%E4%B8%AD%E6%AD%A3%E7%B8%BD%E7%B5%B1%E7%8E%89%E7%85%A7.png,Chiang Kai-shek,china,1925-1949,kuomintang,,Led Kuomintang through warlord conflicts and Japanese invasion. Defeated by Mao. Retreated to Taiwan.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5e/Mao_Zedong_1950_Portrait_%283x4_cropped%29%282%29.jpg,Mao Zedong,china,1949-1976,communist party of china,,"Founded People's Republic of China. Great Leap Forward and Cultural Revolution caused suffering. Early success, later radicalism."
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Deng_Xiaoping_%28cropped%29.jpg,Deng Xiaoping,china,1978-1997,communist party of china,,Transformed China through economic reform. Special economic zones launched rise. Pragmatism over ideology.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Jiang_Zemin_2002_%28cropped%29%282%29.jpg,Jiang Zemin,china,1989-2004,communist party of china,president,Led China through economic liberalization. Economic rise and global integration. Social stability emphasis.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/Xi_Jinping_meets_Putin_May_2026.jpg,Xi Jinping,china,2012-present,communist party of china,president,Current paramount leader. Anti-corruption campaigns. Belt and Road Initiative. Increased authoritarianism.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/Jan_tomb.jpg,King John,england,1199-1216,plantagenet,monarch,Failed military campaigns. Loss of Normandy. Excessive taxation led to Magna Carta signing.
,Henry III,england,1216-1272,plantagenet,monarch,Inherited as child. 56-year reign. Conflict with barons. Development of Parliament.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Edward_I_-_Westminster_Abbey_Sedilia.jpg,Edward I,england,1272-1307,plantagenet,monarch,Conquered Wales. Attempted Scotland conquest. Longshanks and Hammer of the Scots.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Cathedral_Church_of_St_Peter_and_the_Holy_and_Indivisible_Trinity%2C_Monument_to_Edward_II_of_England_Gloucester_1245952_20230819_0030.jpg,Edward II,england,1307-1327,plantagenet,monarch,Military failures. Dependence on favorites. Inability to manage nobility. Forced abdication and murder.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Edward_III_of_England.jpg,Edward III,england,1327-1377,plantagenet,monarch,Established Hundred Years' War against France. Military victories. Founded Order of the Garter.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/The_Westminster_Portrait_of_Richard_II_of_England_%281390s%29.jpg,Richard II,england,1377-1399,plantagenet,monarch,Deposed by Henry Bolingbroke. Established precedent for removing unfit king.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/Illumination_of_Henry_IV_%28cropped%29.jpg,Henry IV,england,1399-1413,lancastrian,monarch,Founded Lancastrian dynasty. Conflict with nobility and Welsh rebellion.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/Henry_V_Miniature.jpg,Henry V,england,1413-1422,plantagenet,monarch,Military genius. Victory at Agincourt. Briefly united English and French thrones.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/Henry_VI_of_England%2C_Shrewsbury_book.jpg,Henry VI,england,1422-1461 & 1470-1471,lancastrian,monarch,Weak rule contributed to Wars of the Roses. Deposition and murder made him tragic figure.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/Edward4.jpg,Edward IV,england,1461-1470 & 1471-1483,yorkist,monarch,Seized throne during Wars of the Roses. Consolidation of Yorkist power. Death left young heirs.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e1/Richard_III_Portrait.png,Richard III,england,1483-1485,yorkist,monarch,Controversial reign. Mysterious death of nephews. Defeated at Bosworth Field. Killed.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/Enrique_VII_de_Inglaterra%2C_por_un_artista_an%C3%B3nimo.jpg,Henry VII,england,1485-1509,tudor,monarch,Founded Tudor dynasty. United Lancasters and Yorkists. Established royal authority.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Henry_VIII_by_Hans_Holbein_the_Younger.jpg,Henry VIII,england,1509-1547,tudor,king,Broke with Rome. Established Church of England. Six marriages. Reshaped religion and politics.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/EdwardVI.jpg,Edward VI,england,1547-1553,tudor,king,Child king. Protestant reforms. Died at 15. Succession crisis.
,Mary I,england,1553-1558,tudor,queen,Attempted Catholic restoration. Religious persecution. Unpopular marriage to Philip II of Spain.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Darnley_stage_3.jpg,Elizabeth I,england,1558-1603,tudor,king,One of Europe's greatest monarchs. Defeated Spanish Armada. Cultural golden age.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/James_I_of_England.jpg,James I,england,1603-1625,stuart,king,United England and Scotland. Divine right of kings believer. Gunpowder Plot. King James Bible.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/King_Charles_I_after_original_by_van_Dyck.jpg,Charles I,england,1625-1649,stuart,king,Conflict with Parliament led to English Civil War. Execution marked transition to parliamentary supremacy.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/Oliver_Cromwell_by_Samuel_Cooper.jpg,Oliver Cromwell,england,1649-1658,commonwealth,prime minister,Led parliamentary forces to victory. Became Lord Protector. Military dictatorship. Puritan ideology.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/Richard_Cromwell_%281626-1712%29%2C_by_Gerard_Soest.jpg,Richard Cromwell,england,1658-1659,commonwealth,prime minister,Inherited position. Lacked father's skill. Unable to manage Parliament. Deposed.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/King_Charles_II_by_John_Michael_Wright_or_studio.jpg,Charles II,england,1660-1685,stuart,king,Restoration of monarchy. Political pragmatism. Balanced religious factions. New political order.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/99/James_II_by_Peter_Lely.jpg,James II,england,1685-1688,stuart,king,Catholic king. Policies interpreted as absolutism attempts. Glorious Revolution deposed him.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/80/Mary_II_-_Kneller_1690.jpg,Mary II,england,1689-1694,stuart,king,Joint monarch with William III. Protestant restoration. Constitutional monarchy symbol.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/78/King_William_III_of_England%2C_%281650-1702%29.jpg,William III,england,1689-1702,orange-nassau,king,Glorious Revolution king. Constitutional transformation and French warfare. Parliamentary reinforcement.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Dahl%2C_Michael_-_Queen_Anne_-_NPG_6187.jpg,Anne,great britain,1702-1714,stuart,queen,Last Stuart monarch. Constitutional transition unifying England and Scotland. War of Spanish Succession.
,George I,great britain,1714-1727,hanover,king,German prince through Protestant succession act. Parliament gained power. Spoke little English.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/George_II_by_Thomas_Hudson.jpg,George II,great britain,1727-1760,hanover,king,Last British monarch to lead troops in battle. British expansion in Americas and India through Seven Years' War.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/George_III_in_Coronation_Robes.jpg,George III,great britain,1760-1820,hanover,king,King during American Revolution and Napoleonic Wars. Mental illness affected later years. Saw American loss and Indian expansion.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/George_IV_of_the_United_Kingdom.jpg,George IV,great britain,1820-1830,hanover,king,First Gentleman of England. Fashion patron. Catholic Emancipation Act. Personal scandals.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/William_IV_%281765-1837%29.jpg,William IV,great britain,1830-1837,hanover,king,Great Reform Act during reign. Expanded voting franchise and parliamentary redistribution.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/Queen_Victoria.jpg,Queen Victoria,great britain,1837-1901,house of saxe-coburg and gotha,emperor,Presided over British Empire at height. Industrialization and imperial expansion. Grandmother of Europe.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/King-Edward-VII_%28cropped%29_%28b%29.jpg,Edward VII,great britain,1901-1910,house of saxe-coburg and gotha,emperor,Peacemaker king. Diplomatic efforts reducing European tensions. Entente Cordiale with France.
,George V,great britain,1910-1936,house of windsor,emperor,Reigned during World War I. Changed house name to Windsor from Saxe-Coburg-Gotha. Rise of constitutional democracy.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/HRH_The_Prince_of_Wales_No_4_%28HS85-10-36416%29.jpg,Edward VIII,great britain,1936,house of windsor,emperor,Abdicated after months on throne. Married Wallis Simpson. Established monarchy serves nation principle.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/80/King_George_VI.jpg,George VI,great britain,1936-1952,house of windsor,emperor,Led Britain through World War II with quiet determination. Earned widespread respect. Restored morale.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Queen_Elizabeth_II_official_portrait_for_1959_tour_%28retouched%29_%28cropped%29_%283-to-4_aspect_ratio%29.jpg,Elizabeth II,great britain,1952-2022,house of windsor,queen,Longest-reigning British monarch. Oversaw transformation of Empire to Commonwealth. Stabilizing force.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/King_Charles_III_%28July_2023%29.jpg,Charles III,great britain,2022-present,house of windsor,monarch,Became king after mother's death. Long experience as Prince of Wales. Modernizing monarchy.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5f/Louis_XIV_of_France.jpg,Louis XIV,france,1643-1715,bourbon,king,Archetypal absolute monarch. Built Versailles. Expanded France to European dominance. Warfare and culture.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Louis_XV_of_France.jpg,Louis XV,france,1715-1774,bourbon,king,Inherited as child. Weak leadership lost dominance to Britain. Warfare depleted treasury.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/Antoine-Fran%C3%A7ois_Callet_-_Louis_XVI%2C_roi_de_France_et_de_Navarre_%281754-1793%29%2C_rev%C3%AAtu_du_grand_costume_royal_en_1779_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg,Louis XVI,france,1774-1792,bourbon,king,Well-intentioned but ineffectual. Financial reform failure. Revolutionary execution marked absolute monarchy's end.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Napol%C3%A9on_Bonaparte.jpg,Napoleon Bonaparte,france,1804-1814 & 1815,bonaparte,emperor,Transformed European politics through military conquest. Napoleonic Code. Administrative reform.
,Louis XVIII,france,1814-1824,bourbon,king,Restored after Napoleon's defeat. Constitutional monarchy. Balanced revolution and reaction.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Carlos_X_de_Francia_%28Fran%C3%A7ois_G%C3%A9rard%29.jpg,Charles X,france,1824-1830,bourbon,king,Conservative monarchist. Undid revolutionary reforms. July Revolution triggered. Overthrown.
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Louis-Philippe_I.jpg,Louis-Philippe I,france,1830-1848,orleans,king,Constitutional monarch. Middle path between monarchy and republicanism. Relatively peaceful and prosperous.
